pe\": \"custom\"}, \"value_cache\": {\"type\": \"custom\"}, \"cache_position\": {\"type\": \"custom\"}, \"q_norm_weight\": {\"type\": \"custom\"}, \"k_norm_weight\": {\"type\": \"custom\"}, \"inv_freq\": {\"type\": \"custom\"}, \"rms_norm_eps\": {\"type\": \"custom\"}}, \"tolerance\": {\"max_atol\": 0.0045000000000000005, \"max_rtol\": 0.05}}\n{\"uuid\": \"13f5b933-268b-5baa-bf48-452cd48dd0e5\", \"axes\": {\"batch_size\": 1, \"seq_len\": 541, \"cache_len\": 0}, \"inputs\": {\"query\": {\"type\": \"custom\"}, \"key\": {\"type\": \"custom\"}, \"value\": {\"type\": \"custom\"}, \"position_ids\": {\"type\": \"custom\"}, \"key_cache\": {\"type\": \"custom\"}, \"value_cache\": {\"type\": \"custom\"}, \"cache_position\": {\"type\": \"custom\"}, \"q_norm_weight\": {\"type\": \"custom\"}, \"k_norm_weight\": {\"type\": \"custom\"}, \"inv_freq\": {\"type\": \"custom\"}, \"rms_norm_eps\": {\"type\": \"custom\"}}, \"tolerance\": {\"max_atol\": 0.0046, \"max_rtol\": 0.05}}\n{\"uuid\": \"2ff5eaf3-5138-5135-b35a-ea14e9014cd2\", \"axes\": {\"batch_size\": 1, \"seq_len\": 1, \"cache_len\": 0}, \"inputs\": {\"query\": {\"type\": \"custom\"}, \"key\": {\"type\": \"custom\"}, \"value\": {\"type\": \"custom\"}, \"position_ids\": {\"type\": \"custom\"}, \"key_cache\": {\"type\": \"custom\"}, \"value_cache\": {\"type\": \"custom\"}, \"cache_position\": {\"type\": \"custom\"}, \"q_norm_weight\": {\"type\": \"custom\"}, \"k_norm_weight\": {\"type\": \"custom\"}, \"inv_freq\": {\"type\": \"custom\"}, \"rms_norm_eps\": {\"type\": \"custom\"}}, \"tolerance\": {\"max_atol\": 1e-05, \"max_rtol\": 0.05}}\n{\"uuid\": \"052e17c9-2fdd-5b2d-a3bc-376d9ffac3e4\", \"axes\": {\"batch_size\": 2, \"seq_len\": 512, \"cache_len\": 256}, \"inputs\": {\"query\": {\"type\": \"custom\"}, \"key\": {\"type\": \"custom\"}, \"value\": {\"type\": \"custom\"}, \"position_ids\": {\"type\": \"custom\"}, \"key_cache\": {\"type\": \"custom\"}, \"value_cache\": {\"type\": \"custom\"}, \"cache_position\": {\"type\": \"custom\"}, \"q_norm_weight\": {\"type\": \"custom\"}, \"k_norm_weight\": {\"type\": \"custom\"}, \"inv_freq\": {\"type\": \"custom\"}, \"rms_norm_eps\": {\"type\": \"custom\"}}, \"tolerance\": {\"max_atol\": 0.0044, \"max_rtol\": 0.05}}\n{\"uuid\": \"39eb0040-eb8e-5cb0-b4f6-130a35f589d9\", \"axes\": {\"batch_size\": 1, \"seq_len\": 2048, \"cache_len\": 0}, \"inputs\": {\"query\": {\"type\": \"custom\"}, \"key\": {\"type\": \"custom\"}, \"value\": {\"type\": \"custom\"}, \"position_ids\": {\"type\": \"custom\"}, \"key_cache\": {\"type\": \"custom\"}, \"value_cache\": {\"type\": \"custom\"}, \"cache_position\": {\"type\": \"custom\"}, \"q_norm_weight\": {\"type\": \"custom\"}, \"k_norm_weight\": {\"type\": \"custom\"}, \"inv_freq\": {\"type\": \"custom\"}, \"rms_norm_eps\": {\"type\": \"custom\"}}, \"tolerance\": {\"max_atol\": 0.0034, \"max_rtol\": 0.05}}\n","numLines":6,"startLine":1,"totalLines":6}}}
{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01J7hyjFef54bgdMj3enzzjr","type":"tool_result","content":"1\t# Draft — L1/018 Fused RoPE + QK-Norm + KV-Cache Update\n2\t\n3\tTarget: NVIDIA **A800 (`sm_80`, Ampere)**. Primary implementation must be **Triton**; PyTorch only\n4\tfor metadata/launch plumbing. No Torch/CPU/NumPy/CUDA-extension computational fallback.\n5\t\n6\t> Skill note: the installed `KernelWiki` and `ncu-report-skill` skills are scoped to Blackwell\n7\t> (SM100/B200) and Hopper (SM90/H100). This task is Ampere `sm_80`, so neither skill is applicable;\n8\t> no skill is consulted for this draft. If profiling on the target were permitted, `ncu` is disallowed\n9\t> by task rules anyway (no direct profiler/CUDA/nvidia-smi runs).\n10\t\n11\t---\n12\t\n13\t## 1. What the operation computes\n14\t\n15\tThe reference (`run`) fuses five logically separate GPU operations into one op:\n16\t\n17\t1. **Per-head RMS normalization** of `query` and `key` over the last axis (`head_dim = 128`).\n18\t2. **RoPE** (rotary position embedding, GPT-NeoX / `rotate_half` variant) applied to the normalized\n19\t   Q and K, using angles derived from `position_ids` and `inv_freq`.\n20\t3. **KV-cache scatter update**: write the rotated K and the (unmodified) V into `key_cache` /\n21\t   `value_cache` at rows given by `cache_position`.\n22\t\n23\t### 1.1 Shapes and dtypes (constants for this task)\n24\t\n25\t| tensor | shape | dtype |\n26\t|---|---|---|\n27\t| `query` | `[B, 96, S, 128]` | bf16 |\n28\t| `key`, `value` | `[B, 8, S, 128]` | bf16 |\n29\t| `position_ids` | `[B, S]` | int64 |\n30\t| `key_cache`, `value_cache` | `[B, 8, 262144, 128]` | bf16 |\n31\t| `cache_position` | `[S]` | int64 |\n32\t| `q_norm_weight`, `k_norm_weight` | `[128]` | bf16 |\n33\t| `inv_freq` | `[64]` | **float32** |\n34\t| `rms_norm_eps` | scalar | float32 (=1e-6) |\n35\t\n36\tConstants: `num_attention_heads = 96`, `num_key_value_heads = 8` (GQA ratio 12),\n37\t`head_dim = 128`, `half_head_dim = 64`, `max_position_embeddings = 262144`,\n38\t`rope_theta = 1e7`, `rms_norm_eps = 1e-6`.\n39\t\n40\tOutputs: `query_rotated [B,96,S,128] bf16`, `key_rotated [B,8,S,128] bf16`, and the **in-place\n41\tupdated** `key_cache`, `value_cache` (returned as-is).\n42\t\n43\t### 1.2 Exact reference math (to replicate bit-closely enough)\n44\t\n45\tRMS norm (note fp32 accumulation, bf16 output):\n46\t```\n47\tx32   = x.float()\n48\tvar   = mean(x32^2, axis=head_dim)          # fp32, divide by 128\n49\txn    = x32 * rsqrt(var + eps)              # fp32\n50\tout   = (weight.float() * xn).to(bf16)      # bf16  <-- rounded here\n51\t```\n52\t\n53\tRoPE angles:\n54\t```\n55\tfreqs = position_ids[b,s].float() * inv_freq[j]    # j in [0,64), fp32\n56\temb   = concat([freqs, freqs])  -> length 128\n57\tcos   = emb.cos().to(bf16)                          # rounded to bf16\n58\tsin   = emb.sin().to(bf16)                          # rounded to bf16\n59\t```\n60\t`rotate_half(x): x1=x[:64], x2=x[64:] -> concat([-x2, x1])`.\n61\t\n62\tapply_rope (broadcast cos/sin across heads):\n63\t```\n64\tout = x * cos + rotate_half(x) * sin\n65\t```\n66\tWritten per pair `(j, j+64)`, with angle `theta_j = pos * inv_freq[j]`:\n67\t```\n68\tout[j]    = xn[j]    * cos(theta_j) - xn[j+64] * sin(theta_j)\n69\tout[j+64] = xn[j+64] * cos(theta_j) + xn[j]    * sin(theta_j)\n70\t```\n71\tSo dims `j` and `j+64` form a 2-D rotation pair (halves paired, **not** adjacent pairs).\n72\t\n73\tCache update (in place, only the `S` selected rows change):\n74\t```\n75\tkey_cache[b, :, cache_position[s], :]   = key_rotated[b, :, s, :]\n76\tvalue_cache[b, :, cache_position[s], :] = value[b, :, s, :]     # V is copied, NOT normed/roped\n77\t```\n78\t`position_ids` drives the **angle**; `cache_position` drives the **cache row index**. They are equal\n79\tin every feedback workload (`arange(cache_len, cache_len+S)`), but the kernel must read them\n80\tindependently for correctness/generality.\n81\t\n82\t---\n83\t\n84\t## 2. Feedback workloads\n85\t\n86\t| # | B | S | cache_len | atol | rtol | character |\n87\t|---|---|---|---|---|---|---|\n88\t| 1 | 1 | 1024 | 512 | 4.5e-3 | 0.05 | prefill-ish, positions 512..1535 |\n89\t| 2 | 1 | 541  | 0   | 4.6e-3 | 0.05 | non-power-of-2 S, positions 0..540 |\n90\t| 3 | 1 | 1    | 0   | **1e-5** | 0.05 | **decode, single token, position 0** |\n91\t| 4 | 2 | 512  | 256 | 4.4e-3 | 0.05 | batch 2, positions 256..767 |\n92\t| 5 | 1 | 2048 | 0   | 3.4e-3 | 0.05 | largest prefill, positions 0..2047 |\n93\t\n94\tObservations that shape design:\n95\t- **Workload 3 (S=1, pos=0)**: `theta = 0` → `cos=1, sin=0` **exactly** → RoPE is the identity, so the\n96\t  output is *purely* the RMS-normed Q/K. The tight `atol=1e-5` is not really binding because `rtol=0.05`\n97\t  on O(1) magnitudes dominates (effective tol ≈ `max(atol, rtol·|ref|)`), but it means the RMS-norm\n98\t  path must be faithful (fp32 variance, correct weight application). No cos/sin rounding error can\n99\t  appear here since the angles are exactly 0.\n100\t- **Mixed regimes**: decode (S=1) vs prefill (S up to 2048). The kernel/launch config must be robust\n101\t  across tiny and large `S` and across non-power-of-2 `S` (541).\n102\t- Positions stay small (≤ 2047), so `pos·inv_freq` is a comfortable fp32 range for `tl.sin/tl.cos`.\n103\t\n104\t---\n105\t\n106\t## 3. Constraints and correctness-critical details\n107\t\n108\t1. **fp32 for the RMS variance.** Never accumulate `x^2` in bf16. Load bf16, cast to fp32, reduce.\n109\t2. **Weight application order.** `(weight.float() * xn)` then round to bf16 — matches reference. Weights\n110\t   are `ones` in these workloads, but read them anyway for generality.\n111\t3. **Halves-paired RoPE**, not adjacent-pairs. Dim `j` pairs with `j+64`; both share `theta_j`.\n112\t4. **V is not transformed** — it is copied verbatim into `value_cache`. Do not norm/rope V.\n113\t5. **In-place cache update; do not disturb other rows.** Only `S` rows per (b, kv_head) are written.\n114\t   The huge cache tensors must be mutated in place and returned as the same objects so unchanged rows\n115\t   stay bit-identical to the input.\n116\t6. **Pointer-offset overflow risk.** Cache is `[B,8,262144,128]`. The base offset for a (b,kv_head)\n117\t   block is `(b*8+h)*262144*128`; for B=2 that reaches `15*262144*128 ≈ 5.03e8`, under int32 max\n118\t   (2.147e9) but uncomfortably close. Compute cache offsets in **int64** to be safe and future-proof.\n119\t   The Q/K/V input tensors are small enough for int32, but I will use int64 for cache indexing.\n120\t7. **Independent index tensors.** Use `position_ids[b,s]` for angle, `cache_position[s]` for the write\n121\t   row. Load both; do not assume equality.\n122\t8. **int64 index loads.** `position_ids` and `cache_position` are int64; load as int, cast to fp32 for\n123\t   the angle and keep int64 for addressing.\n124\t9. **rsqrt semantics.** Reference uses `torch.rsqrt(var+eps)`. Use `tl.rsqrt` (or `1/tl.sqrt`); the\n125\t   difference is negligible vs tolerance.\n126\t10. **Determinism / no aliasing.** `query_rotated` and `key_rotated` are fresh output tensors (allocate\n127\t    with `torch.empty_like`); cache tensors are the passed-in ones.\n128\t\n129\t---\n130\t\n131\t## 4. Numerical-risk analysis\n132\t\n133\t- **cos/sin rounding.** Reference rounds cos/sin to bf16 *before* multiplying; a fused kernel keeps\n134\t  fp32 cos/sin and rounds only the final products. Worst-case per-term deviation from the reference is\n135\t  ≈ `2^-8 · |xn|` ≈ `0.004·|xn|`. With `|xn| = O(1)` and `rtol=0.05` binding on O(1) outputs, this is\n136\t  comfortably inside tolerance. Being *more* precise than the reference is safe because tolerance is\n137\t  measured against the (rounded) reference, and `rtol·|ref|` (~0.05) dwarfs the rounding gap.\n138\t- **xn rounding before RoPE.** Reference feeds bf16 `xn` into RoPE; a fused kernel can keep fp32 `xn`.\n139\t  Same ~0.004 relative envelope, within tolerance. *Optional fidelity knob:* round `xn` to bf16 and\n140\t  back to fp32 before RoPE to emulate the reference more exactly if any workload is borderline. Cheap;\n141\t  keep as a fallback lever, not needed for the first candidate.\n142\t- **Variance precision.** Must be fp32; bf16 accumulation over 128 squared terms would lose too much.\n143\t- **Workload 3 tight atol.** Safe: angles are exactly 0, so the op reduces to faithful RMS norm; the\n144\t  binding constraint is `rtol·|ref|`.\n145\t- **`tl.sin/tl.cos` accuracy.** Triton uses fp32 libdevice; positions ≤ 2047, `inv_freq ≤ 1`, so\n146\t  arguments are small and accuracy is far better than the bf16 target. No range-reduction concerns.\n147\t- **Overflow / NaN.** RMS `var ≥ 0`, `+eps` guards div-by-zero; inputs are `randn` (well-scaled). No\n148\t  overflow expected in bf16 outputs.\n149\t\n150\t---\n151\t\n152\t## 5. Triton design space\n153\t\n154\tThe op is **memory-bound** elementwise + a 128-wide reduction. Q dominates traffic (96 heads vs 8).\n155\tLower-bound traffic per workload ≈ read+write Q (`2·B·96·S·128·2 B`) + read+write K + K-cache write +\n156\tread V + V-cache write. For workload 1 that is ~50 MB for Q alone → tens of µs at ~2 TB/s. Kernels are\n157\ttiny; **occupancy, coalescing, and launch/config overhead dominate**. The big win over the reference is\n158\teliminating the materialization of `cos/sin/emb` (`[B,S,128]` bf16 each) and collapsing 5+ launches.\n159\t\n160\t### 5.1 Kernel decomposition options\n161\t\n162\t- **A — three kernels:** Q (norm+rope), K (norm+rope+cache write), V (copy to cache). Simplest, most\n163\t  coalesced per-kernel; V-copy is a trivial scatter.\n164\t- **B — two kernels:** Q kernel; combined **K+V** kernel where each program handles one\n165\t  `(b, kv_head, seq-block)`: norm+rope K → `key_rotated` + `key_cache`, and copy V → `value_cache`.\n166\t  Fewer launches, shares position/cache-index math. **Preferred baseline.**\n167\t- **C — one kernel:** single launch covering both the 96 Q-heads and 8 K-heads (+V) via a fused grid\n168\t  with per-program role branching. Minimizes launches but complicates indexing (different head counts,\n169\t  cache only for K/V). Consider only if launch overhead proves material for tiny-S workloads.\n170\t\n171\tChosen starting point: **Option B (2 kernels)** — Q kernel and K+V kernel.\n172\t\n173\t### 5.2 Per-program work mapping\n174\t\n175\tWithin a fixed `(b, head)`, the `S` tokens are contiguous rows of 128 (`query` stride: head-dim\n176\tinnermost, then seq). Natural mapping:\n177\t\n178\t- **Program = one block of `R` consecutive seq tokens of one `(b, head)`.**\n179\t  Grid ≈ `(B·H, ceil(S/R))` (or flattened 1-D). Each program:\n180\t  - loads a tile `a = x[:, 0:64]` and `b = x[:, 64:128]` (shape `[R, 64]` each, bf16→fp32),\n181\t  - reduces `sum(a^2)+sum(b^2)` over the 64-axis → per-row variance `[R,1]` (fp32),\n182\t  - normalizes: `an = a*inv*w0`, `bn = b*inv*w1`,\n183\t  - computes `theta[r,j] = pos[r]*inv_freq[j]` → `cos,sin` `[R,64]`,\n184\t  - `out_lo = an*cos - bn*sin`, `out_hi = bn*cos + an*sin`,\n185\t  - stores `out_lo`→cols 0:64, `out_hi`→cols 64:128 as bf16,\n186\t  - (K+V kernel only) scatters `out` to `key_cache[b,h,cache_position[r],:]` and `value` to\n187\t    `value_cache[b,h,cache_position[r],:]`.\n188\t\n189\tThe two-half load makes `rotate_half` free (just reuse `an`/`bn`) and gives a clean `[R,64]` tile with\n190\t`BLOCK_D = 64` matching `half_head_dim`. RMS reduction sums both halves.\n191\t\n192\t### 5.3 Tunable parameters (design space, not committed yet)\n193\t\n194\t- `R` = seq tokens per program (`BLOCK_S`): trade parallelism vs per-program reuse. Candidates 1, 2, 4,\n195\t  8, 16. For S=1 (decode) `R=1` degenerates to one program per (b,head).\n196\t- `num_warps` (1–8) and `num_stages` for the tiny 128-wide inner dim.\n197\t- Grid layout: `(b,head)` outer × seq-block inner vs fully flattened 1-D grid with modular decode.\n198\t- Load width: load full 128 in one `[R,128]` tile then slice halves, vs two `[R,64]` loads. Two loads\n199\t  are conceptually cleaner; a single 128-wide load may coalesce better — compare.\n200\t- Whether to fuse Q+K into one launch (Option C) if launch overhead shows up on S=1 / S=541.\n201\t- Optional `xn→bf16→fp32` fidelity rounding (guarded by a `constexpr` flag).\n202\t\n203\t### 5.4 Baseline first, then optimize\n204\t\n205\tCandidate `c001`: correct, simple **Option B**, `R` modest (e.g. 8), fp32 internal, no autotune — get a\n206\tgreen, measured baseline. Subsequent candidates: autotune `R`/`num_warps`, try single-128-load layout,\n207\ttry Option C fusion, try loading `inv_freq`/weights once, reduce redundant recompute of cos/sin.\n208\t\n209\t---\n210\t\n211\t## 6. Launch / plumbing plan (PyTorch allowed only here)\n212\t\n213\t- Allocate outputs with `torch.empty_like(query)` / `torch.empty_like(key)`.\n214\t- Cache tensors: mutate in place, return the same objects.\n215\t- Derive strides/shapes from tensors; pass scalars (`eps`, `S`, head counts) as kernel args.\n216\t- Compute grid sizes on host from shapes. No torch math on the actual data (no torch cos/sin/rmsnorm) —\n217\t  all computation stays in Triton to honor the \"no Torch computational fallback\" rule and to truly fuse.\n218\t- Handle `S=1` and non-power-of-2 `S` (541) via masked tail loads (`mask = seq_offs < S`).\n219\t\n220\t---\n221\t\n222\t## 7. Validation strategy\n223\t\n224\t1. **Correctness via the official evaluator only** — `./scripts/evaluate_candidate.sh feedback cNNN`\n225\t   over the five fixed workloads. Every selected workload must pass (`atol/rtol` per row above).\n226\t2. **Reasoned pre-checks before each evaluation:**\n227\t   - RoPE pairing is halves-paired (`j`↔`j+64`), matching `rotate_half`.\n228\t   - Variance divides by 128 and is fp32; `+eps` inside the rsqrt.\n229\t   - V copied unchanged; K normed+roped before cache write.\n230\t   - `position_ids` used for angle, `cache_position` for cache row.\n231\t   - Cache offsets computed in int64; only `S` rows touched; other rows untouched (in-place).\n232\t   - Masking correct for `S=541` and `S=1`.\n233\t3. **Regime coverage** is already spanned by the five workloads: decode (3), non-pow2 (2), batch>1 (4),\n234\t   large prefill (5), nonzero cache_len (1,4). No extra harness is permitted or needed.\n235\t4. **Numerical-fidelity fallback:** if a borderline miss appears, enable the `xn→bf16` (and optionally\n236\t   `cos/sin→bf16`) emulation flag to track the reference rounding more exactly — as a *new* candidate ID.\n237\t5. **Performance metric:** geometric-mean speedup vs reference across passing workloads. Expect large\n238\t   gains from removing cos/sin/emb materialization and collapsing 5+ launches into 1–2 fused kernels.\n239\t\n240\t---\n241\t\n242\t## 8. Risks & mitigations (summary)\n243\t\n244\t| risk | mitigation |\n245\t|---|---|\n246\t| bf16 variance loss | fp32 accumulation always |\n247\t| wrong RoPE pairing | halves-paired `(j, j+64)`, unit-check against reference formula |\n248\t| cos/sin rounding drift | rtol=0.05 covers it; optional bf16 emulation flag as fallback candidate |\n249\t| int32 pointer overflow on cache | int64 cache offsets |\n250\t| clobbering untouched cache rows | in-place scatter of only `S` rows; return same tensors |\n251\t| V accidentally transformed | copy V verbatim |\n252\t| tiny S=1 / non-pow2 S | masked loads, robust grid, `R` degrades gracefully |\n253\t| launch overhead on small S | fuse to 2 kernels now; consider single-launch (Option C) later |\n254\t\n255\t---\n256\t\n257\t## 9. Next step\n258\t\n259\tProceed to `docs/plan.md` (executable plan): concrete kernel signatures, grid math, `constexpr`\n260\tparameters, autotune space, and the ordered candidate ladder (`c001` correct baseline → tuned variants).\n261\tNo solution code before the plan is complete.\n262\t"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"3b0d17cb-bc1b-4410-a0c7-0b5b82a14a08","uuid":"c0ab4f78-7a21-4469-8b6e-de175c4c14ef","timestamp":"2026-09-20T03:19:02.001Z","tool_use_result":{"type":"text","file":{"filePath":"/data1/workspace/weihongren/kda-runs/formal-kda-20260916--sol_execbench--L1-018_fused_rope_with_qk_norm_and_kv_cache_update/docs/draft.md","content":"# Draft — L1/018 Fused RoPE + QK-Norm + KV-Cache Update\n\nTarget: NVIDIA **A800 (`sm_80`, Ampere)**. Primary implementation must be **Triton**; PyTorch only\nfor metadata/launch plumbing. No Torch/CPU/NumPy/CUDA-extension computational fallback.\n\n> Skill note: the installed `KernelWiki` and `ncu-report-skill` skills are scoped to Blackwell\n> (SM100/B200) and Hopper (SM90/H100). This task is Ampere `sm_80`, so neither skill is applicable;\n> no skill is consulted for this draft. If profiling on the target were permitted, `ncu` is disallowed\n> by task rules anyway (no direct profiler/CUDA/nvidia-smi runs).\n\n---\n\n## 1. What the operation computes\n\nThe reference (`run`) fuses five logically separate GPU operations into one op:\n\n1. **Per-head RMS normalization** of `query` and `key` over the last axis (`head_dim = 128`).\n2. **RoPE** (rotary position embedding, GPT-NeoX / `rotate_half` variant) applied to the normalized\n   Q and K, using angles derived from `position_ids` and `inv_freq`.\n3. **KV-cache scatter update**: write the rotated K and the (unmodified) V into `key_cache` /\n   `value_cache` at rows given by `cache_position`.\n\n### 1.1 Shapes and dtypes (constants for this task)\n\n| tensor | shape | dtype |\n|---|---|---|\n| `query` | `[B, 96, S, 128]` | bf16 |\n| `key`, `value` | `[B, 8, S, 128]` | bf16 |\n| `position_ids` | `[B, S]` | int64 |\n| `key_cache`, `value_cache` | `[B, 8, 262144, 128]` | bf16 |\n| `cache_position` | `[S]` | int64 |\n| `q_norm_weight`, `k_norm_weight` | `[128]` | bf16 |\n| `inv_freq` | `[64]` | **float32** |\n| `rms_norm_eps` | scalar | float32 (=1e-6) |\n\nConstants: `num_attention_heads = 96`, `num_key_value_heads = 8` (GQA ratio 12),\n`head_dim = 128`, `half_head_dim = 64`, `max_position_embeddings = 262144`,\n`rope_theta = 1e7`, `rms_norm_eps = 1e-6`.\n\nOutputs: `query_rotated [B,96,S,128] bf16`, `key_rotated [B,8,S,128] bf16`, and the **in-place\nupdated** `key_cache`, `value_cache` (returned as-is).\n\n### 1.2 Exact reference math (to replicate bit-closely enough)\n\nRMS norm (note fp32 accumulation, bf16 output):\n```\nx32   = x.float()\nvar   = mean(x32^2, axis=head_dim)          # fp32, divide by 128\nxn    = x32 * rsqrt(var + eps)              # fp32\nout   = (weight.float() * xn).to(bf16)      # bf16  <-- rounded here\n```\n\nRoPE angles:\n```\nfreqs = position_ids[b,s].float() * inv_freq[j]    # j in [0,64), fp32\nemb   = concat([freqs, freqs])  -> length 128\ncos   = emb.cos().to(bf16)                          # rounded to bf16\nsin   = emb.sin().to(bf16)                          # rounded to bf16\n```\n`rotate_half(x): x1=x[:64], x2=x[64:] -> concat([-x2, x1])`.\n\napply_rope (broadcast cos/sin across heads):\n```\nout = x * cos + rotate_half(x) * sin\n```\nWritten per pair `(j, j+64)`, with angle `theta_j = pos * inv_freq[j]`:\n```\nout[j]    = xn[j]    * cos(theta_j) - xn[j+64] * sin(theta_j)\nout[j+64] = xn[j+64] * cos(theta_j) + xn[j]    * sin(theta_j)\n```\nSo dims `j` and `j+64` form a 2-D rotation pair (halves paired, **not** adjacent pairs).\n\nCache update (in place, only the `S` selected rows change):\n```\nkey_cache[b, :, cache_position[s], :]   = key_rotated[b, :, s, :]\nvalue_cache[b, :, cache_position[s], :] = value[b, :, s, :]     # V is copied, NOT normed/roped\n```\n`position_ids` drives the **angle**; `cache_position` drives the **cache row index**. They are equal\nin every feedback workload (`arange(cache_len, cache_len+S)`), but the kernel must read them\nindependently for correctness/generality.\n\n---\n\n## 2. Feedback workloads\n\n| # | B | S | cache_len | atol | rtol | character |\n|---|---|---|---|---|---|---|\n| 1 | 1 | 1024 | 512 | 4.5e-3 | 0.05 | prefill-ish, positions 512..1535 |\n| 2 | 1 | 541  | 0   | 4.6e-3 | 0.05 | non-power-of-2 S, positions 0..540 |\n| 3 | 1 | 1    | 0   | **1e-5** | 0.05 | **decode, single token, position 0** |\n| 4 | 2 | 512  | 256 | 4.4e-3 | 0.05 | batch 2, positions 256..767 |\n| 5 | 1 | 2048 | 0   | 3.4e-3 | 0.05 | largest prefill, positions 0..2047 |\n\nObservations that shape design:\n- **Workload 3 (S=1, pos=0)**: `theta = 0` → `cos=1, sin=0` **exactly** → RoPE is the identity, so the\n  output is *purely* the RMS-normed Q/K. The tight `atol=1e-5` is not really binding because `rtol=0.05`\n  on O(1) magnitudes dominates (effective tol ≈ `max(atol, rtol·|ref|)`), but it means the RMS-norm\n  path must be faithful (fp32 variance, correct weight application). No cos/sin rounding error can\n  appear here since the angles are exactly 0.\n- **Mixed regimes**: decode (S=1) vs prefill (S up to 2048). The kernel/launch config must be robust\n  across tiny and large `S` and across non-power-of-2 `S` (541).\n- Positions stay small (≤ 2047), so `pos·inv_freq` is a comfortable fp32 range for `tl.sin/tl.cos`.\n\n---\n\n## 3. Constraints and correctness-critical details\n\n1. **fp32 for the RMS variance.** Never accumulate `x^2` in bf16. Load bf16, cast to fp32, reduce.\n2. **Weight application order.** `(weight.float() * xn)` then round to bf16 — matches reference. Weights\n   are `ones` in these workloads, but read them anyway for generality.\n3. **Halves-paired RoPE**, not adjacent-pairs. Dim `j` pairs with `j+64`; both share `theta_j`.\n4. **V is not transformed** — it is copied verbatim into `value_cache`. Do not norm/rope V.\n5. **In-place cache update; do not disturb other rows.** Only `S` rows per (b, kv_head) are written.\n   The huge cache tensors must be mutated in place and returned as the same objects so unchanged rows\n   stay bit-identical to the input.\n6. **Pointer-offset overflow risk.** Cache is `[B,8,262144,128]`. The base offset for a (b,kv_head)\n   block is `(b*8+h)*262144*128`; for B=2 that reaches `15*262144*128 ≈ 5.03e8`, under int32 max\n   (2.147e9) but uncomfortably close. Compute cache offsets in **int64** to be safe and future-proof.\n   The Q/K/V input tensors are small enough for int32, but I will use int64 for cache indexing.\n7. **Independent index tensors.** Use `position_ids[b,s]` for angle, `cache_position[s]` for the write\n   row. Load both; do not assume equality.\n8. **int64 index loads.** `position_ids` and `cache_position` are int64; load as int, cast to fp32 for\n   the angle and keep int64 for addressing.\n9. **rsqrt semantics.** Reference uses `torch.rsqrt(var+eps)`. Use `tl.rsqrt` (or `1/tl.sqrt`); the\n   difference is negligible vs tolerance.\n10. **Determinism / no aliasing.** `query_rotated` and `key_rotated` are fresh output tensors (allocate\n    with `torch.empty_like`); cache tensors are the passed-in ones.\n\n---\n\n## 4. Numerical-risk analysis\n\n- **cos/sin rounding.** Reference rounds cos/sin to bf16 *before* multiplying; a fused kernel keeps\n  fp32 cos/sin and rounds only the final products. Worst-case per-term deviation from the reference is\n  ≈ `2^-8 · |xn|` ≈ `0.004·|xn|`. With `|xn| = O(1)` and `rtol=0.05` binding on O(1) outputs, this is\n  comfortably inside tolerance. Being *more* precise than the reference is safe because tolerance is\n  measured against the (rounded) reference, and `rtol·|ref|` (~0.05) dwarfs the rounding gap.\n- **xn rounding before RoPE.** Reference feeds bf16 `xn` into RoPE; a fused kernel can keep fp32 `xn`.\n  Same ~0.004 relative envelope, within tolerance. *Optional fidelity knob:* round `xn` to bf16 and\n  back to fp32 before RoPE to emulate the reference more exactly if any workload is borderline. Cheap;\n  keep as a fallback lever, not needed for the first candidate.\n- **Variance precision.** Must be fp32; bf16 accumulation over 128 squared terms would lose too much.\n- **Workload 3 tight atol.** Safe: angles are exactly 0, so the op reduces to faithful RMS norm; the\n  binding constraint is `rtol·|ref|`.\n- **`tl.sin/tl.cos` accuracy.** Triton uses fp32 libdevice; positions ≤ 2047, `inv_freq ≤ 1`, so\n  arguments are small and accuracy is far better than the bf16 target. No range-reduction concerns.\n- **Overflow / NaN.** RMS `var ≥ 0`, `+eps` guards div-by-zero; inputs are `randn` (well-scaled). No\n  overflow expected in bf16 outputs.\n\n---\n\n## 5. Triton design space\n\nThe op is **memory-bound** elementwise + a 128-wide reduction. Q dominates traffic (96 heads vs 8).\nLower-bound traffic per workload ≈ read+write Q (`2·B·96·S·128·2 B`) + read+write K + K-cache write +\nread V + V-cache write. For workload 1 that is ~50 MB for Q alone → tens of µs at ~2 TB/s. Kernels are\ntiny; **occupancy, coalescing, and launch/config overhead dominate**. The big win over the reference is\neliminating the materialization of `cos/sin/emb` (`[B,S,128]` bf16 each) and collapsing 5+ launches.\n\n### 5.1 Kernel decomposition options\n\n- **A — three kernels:** Q (norm+rope), K (norm+rope+cache write), V (copy to cache). Simplest, most\n  coalesced per-kernel; V-copy is a trivial scatter.\n- **B — two kernels:** Q kernel; combined **K+V** kernel where each program handles one\n  `(b, kv_head, seq-block)`: norm+rope K → `key_rotated` + `key_cache`, and copy V → `value_cache`.\n  Fewer launches, shares position/cache-index math. **Preferred baseline.**\n- **C — one kernel:** single launch covering both the 96 Q-heads and 8 K-heads (+V) via a fused grid\n  with per-program role branching. Minimizes launches but complicates indexing (different head counts,\n  cache only for K/V). Consider only if launch overhead proves material for tiny-S workloads.\n\nChosen starting point: **Option B (2 kernels)** — Q kernel and K+V kernel.\n\n### 5.2 Per-program work mapping\n\nWithin a fixed `(b, head)`, the `S` tokens are contiguous rows of 128 (`query` stride: head-dim\ninnermost, then seq). Natural mapping:\n\n- **Program = one block of `R` consecutive seq tokens of one `(b, head)`.**\n  Grid ≈ `(B·H, ceil(S/R))` (or flattened 1-D). Each program:\n  - loads a tile `a = x[:, 0:64]` and `b = x[:, 64:128]` (shape `[R, 64]` each, bf16→fp32),\n  - reduces `sum(a^2)+sum(b^2)` over the 64-axis → per-row variance `[R,1]` (fp32),\n  - normalizes: `an = a*inv*w0`, `bn = b*inv*w1`,\n  - computes `theta[r,j] = pos[r]*inv_freq[j]` → `cos,sin` `[R,64]`,\n  - `out_lo = an*cos - bn*sin`, `out_hi = bn*cos + an*sin`,\n  - stores `out_lo`→cols 0:64, `out_hi`→cols 64:128 as bf16,\n  - (K+V kernel only) scatters `out` to `key_cache[b,h,cache_position[r],:]` and `value` to\n    `value_cache[b,h,cache_position[r],:]`.\n\nThe two-half load makes `rotate_half` free (just reuse `an`/`bn`) and gives a clean `[R,64]` tile with\n`BLOCK_D = 64` matching `half_head_dim`. RMS reduction sums both halves.\n\n### 5.3 Tunable parameters (design space, not committed yet)\n\n- `R` = seq tokens per program (`BLOCK_S`): trade parallelism vs per-program reuse. Candidates 1, 2, 4,\n  8, 16. For S=1 (decode) `R=1` degenerates to one program per (b,head).\n- `num_warps` (1–8) and `num_stages` for the tiny 128-wide inner dim.\n- Grid layout: `(b,head)` outer × seq-block inner vs fully flattened 1-D grid with modular decode.\n- Load width: load full 128 in one `[R,128]` tile then slice halves, vs two `[R,64]` loads. Two loads\n  are conceptually cleaner; a single 128-wide load may coalesce better — compare.\n- Whether to fuse Q+K into one launch (Option C) if launch overhead shows up on S=1 / S=541.\n- Optional `xn→bf16→fp32` fidelity rounding (guarded by a `constexpr` flag).\n\n### 5.4 Baseline first, then optimize\n\nCandidate `c001`: correct, simple **Option B**, `R` modest (e.g. 8), fp32 internal, no autotune — get a\ngreen, measured baseline. Subsequent candidates: autotune `R`/`num_warps`, try single-128-load layout,\ntry Option C fusion, try loading `inv_freq`/weights once, reduce redundant recompute of cos/sin.\n\n---\n\n## 6. Launch / plumbing plan (PyTorch allowed only here)\n\n- Allocate outputs with `torch.empty_like(query)` / `torch.empty_like(key)`.\n- Cache tensors: mutate in place, return the same objects.\n- Derive strides/shapes from tensors; pass scalars (`eps`, `S`, head counts) as kernel args.\n- Compute grid sizes on host from shapes. No torch math on the actual data (no torch cos/sin/rmsnorm) —\n  all computation stays in Triton to honor the \"no Torch computational fallback\" rule and to truly fuse.\n- Handle `S=1` and non-power-of-2 `S` (541) via masked tail loads (`mask = seq_offs < S`).\n\n---\n\n## 7. Validation strategy\n\n1. **Correctness via the official evaluator only** — `./scripts/evaluate_candidate.sh feedback cNNN`\n   over the five fixed workloads. Every selected workload must pass (`atol/rtol` per row above).\n2. **Reasoned pre-checks before each evaluation:**\n   - RoPE pairing is halves-paired (`j`↔`j+64`), matching `rotate_half`.\n   - Variance divides by 128 and is fp32; `+eps` inside the rsqrt.\n   - V copied unchanged; K normed+roped before cache write.\n   - `position_ids` used for angle, `cache_position` for cache row.\n   - Cache offsets computed in int64; only `S` rows touched; other rows untouched (in-place).\n   - Masking correct for `S=541` and `S=1`.\n3. **Regime coverage** is already spanned by the five workloads: decode (3), non-pow2 (2), batch>1 (4),\n   large prefill (5), nonzero cache_len (1,4). No extra harness is permitted or needed.\n4. **Numerical-fidelity fallback:** if a borderline miss appears, enable the `xn→bf16` (and optionally\n   `cos/sin→bf16`) emulation flag to track the reference rounding more exactly — as a *new* candidate ID.\n5. **Performance metric:** geometric-mean speedup vs reference across passing workloads. Expect large\n   gains from removing cos/sin/emb materialization and collapsing 5+ launches into 1–2 fused kernels.\n\n---\n\n## 8. Risks & mitigations (summary)\n\n| risk | mitigation |\n|---|---|\n| bf16 variance loss | fp32 accumulation always |\n| wrong RoPE pairing | halves-paired `(j, j+64)`, unit-check against reference formula |\n| cos/sin rounding drift | rtol=0.05 covers it; optional bf16 emulation flag as fallback candidate |\n| int32 pointer overflow on cache | int64 cache offsets |\n| clobbering untouched cache rows | in-place scatter of only `S` rows; return same tensors |\n| V accidentally transformed | copy V verbatim |\n| tiny S=1 / non-pow2 S | masked loads, robust grid, `R` degrades gracefully |\n| launch overhead on small S | fuse to 2 kernels now; consider single-launch (Option C) later |\n\n---\n\n## 9. Next step\n\nProceed to `docs/plan.md` (executable plan): concrete kernel signatures, grid math, `constexpr`\nparameters, autotune space, and the ordered candidate ladder (`c001` correct baseline → tuned variants).\nNo solution code before the plan is complete.\n","numLines":262,"startLine":1,"totalLines":262}}}
